r  actually we do have a forum at sky where  where women can get together and and  talk about the menopause and that makes  a big difference um for women of a  certain age actually you can go through  the menopause younger as well of course  um but it's important for younger people  men and women to realize what the  menopause is i call it the power surge  because menopause was really a dirty  word for a long time but i think i think  the tide is turning isn't it  a hundred percent things are definitely  changing you know we are seeing some  organizations doing some excellent work  in this space but what we need is we  need that across the board because  essentially  if somebody steps away from the  workplace before they have reached their  true potential that's tragic that's  tragic for the individual also obviously  can have a financial impact on their  family situation but it's also tragic  for the employer because they're losing  a valuable member of their team  the you know sort of the answers really  are quite simple and it is absolutely a  win-win situation  dan we must leave it there but it's  great to talk to you thanks so much for  taking the time thank you pleasure  thanks kate thanks a lot  as of today people in england who test  positive for coronavirus will only be  required to isolate for five full days  as long as they test negative on the  fifth and the sixth day or certainly the  morning of the sixth day joining us now  is dr david nabarro from the world  health organization hi doctor thank you  for joining us do you um think this is a  good idea  i think that  every government now has to be dealing  with just one  really major challenge and that is an  awful lot of people are  sick with kobe right now  and that means a lot of absenteeism  and so each government has to make a  trade-off which is the greater risk to  have uh further collapse of essential  services like hospitals  and  supermarkets and the like because of  people just being absent for long  periods or shorten the period of absence  accept the risk that some may still be  infectious but the majority won't be at  the end of a shorter period of isolation  and try to keep society going and i  watch these choices being made i  completely understand them and i think  that as long as there is just one thing  done and that is that people are told  that we're still dealing with a virus  about which we know very little and it  may be necessary if we don't get things  right the first time perhaps to adjust  the the instructions and change the  restrictions but right now the plan is  to try to shorten isolation and to see  if that can reduce the absenteeism  problem  and to the other end of the scale doctor  what about long coverage what do we know  about that how do we know whether it's  long covered or whether it's lethargy  and brain fog as a result of of other  ailments or indeed the season  it's been always been a challenge to  work out for certain whether the longer  symptoms that sometimes occur uh during  and after a viral illness  are what some would call a genuine  illness and what others would say is  perhaps something that's uh due to one's  attitude and mood i've always been a  believer that there is such a thing as a  prolonged illness after viruses and  we're seeing exactly that with kovid  long cove it is for real some say it's  one in 20 getting it others say it's  perhaps one in 31 in 40 but whatever it  is and many people do have prolonged  symptoms that can last up to three  months even longer and does omicron give  us the same long covid as other forms of  the virus do my colleagues who work on  long covid say that preliminary signs  suggest that it it is happening so it  just leaves me to say to everybody  this virus is horrible it has so many  effects in so many different ways long  covert is definitely one of them  okay um but for most  of us most people who have had  covid it does  appear from the latest figures at the  end is in sight we're down from 120 000  cases on sunday uh to 81 000 cases  yesterday that in a week down by a third  in the uk  well i mean first things first you know  this is the uk story but really  elsewhere in the world things are  nothing like as promising  we have  many many reports of countries where  health services are overwhelmed because  of illness combination of delta and  omicron so looking at it globally  there's still a long way to go but  looking at it from a uk point of view  yeah there does appear to be light at  the end of the tunnel all i'm saying to  everybody is i think that it's going to  be bumpy before we get to the end so  even though it's possible to start  imagining that the end of the pandemic  is is is not far away  just everybody be ready for the  possibility that there will be more  variations and mutations coming along or  that there will be further challenges  with other surges even of omicron coming  now don't imagine that it's all going to  be fine i know that sounds like i'm a  killjoy i'm trying not to be i'm just  simply sharing my own emotions about  this  um i read over the weekend that more and  more young children are in hospital in  london with covid how do we protect our  little ones of course something close to  you  yes  yeah i mean we talked a lot about my  grandchildren i just found out over the  weekend that in fact one of my  grandchildren a young lad called max  who's is still i mean he's just over one  and he's got covid with his mum and  they've been isolating and it's super  hard to protect children i think this is  just one of the many really troublesome  features about this disease is the  children get it they don't get very ill  but they are perfectly capable of  passing it on and we're going to go on  having children actually acting as the  vectors of the virus for some time to  come and that means of course that there  will be some children who get severely  ill and i noticed as you have that there  are increasing numbers of children in  hospital with some consequences of their  covet i've got nothing unfortunate that  i can say about this as a specific point  but it just helps me make my general  point which is that we should all of us  be still be respectful of this virus do  what you can to stop transmitting it do  what you can to protect others from  being affected by it it's not the common  cold i know people would like it to be  it's a it's a virus that has still some  really unpleasant features let's do our  best to protect people from it if we  possibly can  well we all wish max better with his mum  and hopefully that happens very soon  thank you very much indeed doctor thank  you thank you thanks a lot
 

